% Generated by hand; maintained manually in man/sobol_pts.Rd
\name{sobol_pts}
\alias{sobol_pts}
\docType{data}
\title{Sobol Low-Discrepancy Point Set}
\description{
  A data frame of 100 two-dimensional points generated from a Sobol
  low-discrepancy sequence. The points fill the unit square
  \eqn{[0, 1] \times [0, 1]} more evenly than independent uniform random
  samples, yet are fixed across sessions. This makes them suitable as
  consistent, reproducible input for plotting and graphical examples
  throughout the package that require a set of points that \emph{look}
  somewhat random without varying between calls.
}
\usage{data(sobol_pts)}
\format{
  A data frame with 100 rows and 2 columns:
  \describe{
    \item{\code{x}}{Numeric. The x-coordinate of each point, in
      \eqn{[0, 1]}.}
    \item{\code{y}}{Numeric. The y-coordinate of each point, in
      \eqn{[0, 1]}.}
  }
}
\details{
  The sequence was produced by
  \code{randtoolbox::sobol(100, 2)} with default settings (scrambling
  off, starting from the first point). Sobol sequences are a type of
  quasi-random (low-discrepancy) sequence: consecutive points avoid
  clustering and fill space more uniformly than pseudo-random numbers,
  while remaining completely deterministic.

  Because the points are stored as package data rather than generated at
  run time, they are identical in every R session regardless of the
  current random-number seed.
}
\source{
  Generated using the \pkg{randtoolbox} package:

  Christophe Dutang and Petr Savicky (2024). \pkg{randtoolbox}: Toolbox
  for Pseudo and Quasi Random Number Generation and Random Generator
  Tests. R package. \url{https://CRAN.R-project.org/package=randtoolbox}

  The underlying Sobol sequence algorithm is based on:

  Bratley, P. and Fox, B. L. (1988). Algorithm 659: Implementing
  Sobol's quasirandom sequence generator.
  \emph{ACM Transactions on Mathematical Software}, \strong{14}(1),
  88--100. \doi{10.1145/42288.214372}

  Joe, S. and Kuo, F. Y. (2003). Remark on Algorithm 659: Implementing
  Sobol's quasirandom sequence generator.
  \emph{ACM Transactions on Mathematical Software}, \strong{29}(1),
  49--57. \doi{10.1145/641876.641879}
}
\seealso{
  \code{\link[randtoolbox]{sobol}} for the function used to generate
  this data set and for options such as scrambling and starting index.
}
\examples{
data(sobol_pts)
plot(sobol_pts$x, sobol_pts$y, pch = 19, asp = 1,
     xlab = "x", ylab = "y",
     main = "Sobol low-discrepancy points")
}
